#{[17592186045463 #edb/ref 10 :activity.type/lesson #edb/ref 17592186045416] [17592186045486 #edb/ref 10 :question.difficulty/hard #edb/ref 17592186045416] [17592186045487 #edb/ref 10 :question.difficulty/moderate #edb/ref 17592186045416] [17592186045492 #edb/ref 10 :answer-field.type/blank #edb/ref 17592186045416] [17592186045493 #edb/ref 10 :answer-field.type/radio #edb/ref 17592186045416] [17592186045498 #edb/ref 10 :answer.type/math #edb/ref 17592186045416] [17592186046658 #edb/ref 1004 #edb/ref 17592186046201 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046658 #edb/ref 1004 #edb/ref 17592186046202 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046658 #edb/ref 1004 #edb/ref 17592186046203 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046658 #edb/ref 1004 #edb/ref 17592186046204 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046658 #edb/ref 1004 #edb/ref 17592186046205 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046658 #edb/ref 1004 #edb/ref 17592186046206 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046658 #edb/ref 1004 #edb/ref 17592186046207 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046658 #edb/ref 1004 #edb/ref 17592186046208 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046658 #edb/ref 1004 #edb/ref 17592186046209 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046658 #edb/ref 1004 #edb/ref 17592186046210 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046658 #edb/ref 1004 #edb/ref 17592186046211 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046658 #edb/ref 1004 #edb/ref 17592186046212 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046658 #edb/ref 1004 #edb/ref 17592186046213 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046658 #edb/ref 1004 #edb/ref 17592186046214 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046658 #edb/ref 1004 #edb/ref 17592186046215 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046658 #edb/ref 1004 #edb/ref 17592186046216 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046658 #edb/ref 1004 #edb/ref 17592186046217 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046658 #edb/ref 1004 #edb/ref 17592186046218 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046658 #edb/ref 1004 #edb/ref 17592186046219 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046658 #edb/ref 1004 #edb/ref 17592186046220 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046658 #edb/ref 1004 #edb/ref 17592186046221 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046658 #edb/ref 1004 #edb/ref 17592186046222 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046658 #edb/ref 1004 #edb/ref 17592186046223 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046658 #edb/ref 1004 #edb/ref 17592186046224 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046658 #edb/ref 1004 #edb/ref 17592186046225 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046658 #edb/ref 1004 #edb/ref 17592186046226 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046658 #edb/ref 1004 #edb/ref 17592186046227 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046658 #edb/ref 1004 #edb/ref 17592186046228 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046658 #edb/ref 1005 #edb/ref 17592186046704 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046658 #edb/ref 1005 #edb/ref 17592186046721 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046658 #edb/ref 1005 #edb/ref 17592186046728 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046658 #edb/ref 1005 #edb/ref 17592186046741 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046658 #edb/ref 1005 #edb/ref 17592186046762 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046658 #edb/ref 1005 #edb/ref 17592186046793 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046658 #edb/ref 1005 #edb/ref 17592186046836 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046658 #edb/ref 1005 #edb/ref 17592186046870 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046658 #edb/ref 1005 #edb/ref 17592186046875 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046658 #edb/ref 1005 #edb/ref 17592186046893 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046658 #edb/ref 1005 #edb/ref 17592186046941 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046658 #edb/ref 1005 #edb/ref 17592186046975 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046658 #edb/ref 1007 "Mathematics for Machine Learning" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046658 #edb/ref 1008 "Our Mathematics for Machine Learning course provides a comprehensive foundation of the essential mathematical tools required to study modern machine learning.\nThis course is divided into three main categories: linear algebra, multivariable calculus, and probability & statistics. The linear algebra section covers crucial machine learning fundamentals such as matrices, vector spaces, diagonalization, projections, singular value decomposition, and regression, as well as dimensionality reduction techniques such as principal component analysis. The multivariable calculus section develops the tools needed for optimization and learning algorithms, including vector and matrix calculus. Finally, the probability and statistics section covers random variables, point estimation, maximum likelihood estimation, and confidence intervals.\nOn completing this course, students will be well-prepared for a university-level machine learning course that tackles concepts such as gradient descent, neural networks, backpropagation, support vector machines, naive Bayes classifiers, and Gaussian mixture models." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046658 #edb/ref 1009 "The course begins with foundational topics in set theory and logic. Students develop a precise mathematical language for describing sets, functions, and predicates, and are introduced to key ideas such as supremum and infimum, as well as argmax and argmin notation. Logical reasoning and Boolean functions are also studied, providing important conceptual motivation for understanding the expressive limitations of some simple machine learning models, such as why certain functions (e.g. XOR) cannot be represented by a single-layer neural network.\nStudents then explore matrices in-depth. They study Gaussian elimination, solve systems of equations, learn about determinants and their properties, and compute inverse matrices. These tools form the computational backbone of many machine learning algorithms.\nAs part of this course, students perform a deep dive into vector spaces, exploring linear independence, subspaces, bases, dimension, rank, and nullity. They also study operations such as the Hadamard product, which appears naturally in element-wise computations in neural networks. Students generalize key concepts to abstract vector spaces and inner product spaces, and study orthogonality, projections, and the Gram–Schmidt process.\nStudents will learn how to find eigenvectors of a matrix, compute matrix diagonalizations, and extend these ideas to orthogonal diagonalization of symmetric matrices. These techniques underpin dimensionality reduction methods and are central to understanding the structure of data.\nThe course then develops key applications of linear algebra in machine learning. Students study singular value decomposition and its relationship to principal component analysis, and learn how these tools are used for dimensionality reduction, feature extraction, and identifying structure in high-dimensional datasets. Linear least-squares problems and regression techniques are also studied as foundational methods for fitting models to data.\nA solid grasp of multivariable calculus is essential for understanding modern machine learning algorithms. In this course, students become well-versed in partial derivatives and gradient vectors (used in gradient-based optimization), the multivariable chain rule (essential for backpropagation), and vector-valued functions. Students extend differentiation to maps between multi-dimensional vector spaces and develop the tools of vector and matrix calculus used to compute gradients of loss functions in neural networks.\nStudents also study optimization of multivariable functions, including critical points, second derivative tests, and constrained optimization using Lagrange multipliers. These techniques form the mathematical foundation of algorithms such as support vector machines and constrained optimization problems in machine learning.\nStudents work with standard multivariable surfaces to build intuition for loss surfaces, and study double integrals as a tool for understanding continuous probability distributions.\nOn the probability and statistics side, students study discrete and continuous random variables, including probability density functions, cumulative distribution functions, transformations, expectation, moments, variance, and Bayes' theorem. Important probability distributions are explored in detail.\nStudents extend their understanding to joint, marginal, and conditional distributions, as well as sums and products of random variables. Topics such as covariance, correlation, and multivariate normal distributions are introduced, which are essential in probabilistic modeling.\nThe course concludes with parametric inference, focusing on point estimation, maximum likelihood estimation, and confidence intervals, which form the statistical foundation for many machine learning methods." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046658 #edb/ref 1012 145 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046658 #edb/ref 1013 "MML" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046658 #edb/ref 1014 #uuid "1ee987ec-c9ff-5d2f-91f4-4de8079c0eac" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186048675 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186188348 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186048675 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186188352 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186048675 #edb/ref 1031 "Simulating Random Observations" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186048675 #edb/ref 1033 3867 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186048675 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "b3cee784-9b06-5621-9585-777c322559ad" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186048871 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186161997 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186048871 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186161998 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186048871 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186161999 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186048871 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186162000 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186048871 #edb/ref 1031 "Median, Quartiles and Percentiles of Continuous Random Variables" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186048871 #edb/ref 1033 3024 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186048871 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "c80985c0-d51a-5f21-9b71-21587d8d82cc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049553 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186048466 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049553 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186050682 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049553 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186175999 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049553 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186176000 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049553 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186176001 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049553 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186176037 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049553 #edb/ref 1031 "Marginal Distributions for Continuous Random Variables" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049553 #edb/ref 1033 3636 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049553 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "fed8aeb7-cb56-5ca6-9352-ed88fd02f45e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049638 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186048744 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049638 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186105860 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049638 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186105861 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049638 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186105886 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049638 #edb/ref 1031 "Probability Density Functions of Continuous Random Variables" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049638 #edb/ref 1033 1347 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049638 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "0748b9c2-fae7-5522-9655-db9e6f1d50c0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049956 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186048724 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049956 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186050219 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049956 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186162046 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049956 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186162047 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049956 #edb/ref 1031 "The Distribution Function Method" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049956 #edb/ref 1033 3055 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049956 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "21353370-ac76-5b2e-8404-7422bec4c2e7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050750 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186048675 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050750 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186048871 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050750 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186049553 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050750 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186049956 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050750 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186133726 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050750 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186133727 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050750 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186133728 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050750 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186133729 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050750 #edb/ref 1031 "Cumulative Distribution Functions for Continuous Random Variables" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050750 #edb/ref 1033 2163 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050750 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "6390206c-1add-5ea0-84f9-8288f56f20b5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186131063 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186419908 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186131063 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186131063 #edb/ref 1077 "field-1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186131063 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186419908 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186131063 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "13407dc0-5b13-5eaa-91b6-bca274b27437" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133516 #edb/ref 1069 "e-13362" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133516 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that the random variable $X$ has the probability density function\n![](images/17/1714eb4797f7b318f5e957db7ab0f322fffb2eb2c9bf03f68e3acc08ec399849.svg)\n\nfind the cumulative distribution function $F(x).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133516 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "38b4473a-f956-5af5-863b-387dc10b4924" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133516 #edb/ref 1074 "Since our random variable $X$ has a probability distribution function $f(x)$ that is strictly positive only for $x \\geq 0,$ the cumulative distribution function takes the following form:\n\n![](images/4f/4f0d336b322f68550c50d65380e26ffb33bdd55beab0c7d429e7b4055f973b57.svg)\n\nFor the given probability density function, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{x}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\int }_{0}^{x}{e}^{-t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\text{ } & =-{e}^{-t}{|}_{0}^{x} \\\\  & =-{e}^{-x}+1 \\\\  & =1-{e}^{-x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the cumulative distribution function is given by\n\n![](images/9e/9e1b63a37177781672b70c3a6c01b1b752164b976b6e9982e07d510d468353aa.svg)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133517 #edb/ref 1069 "e-13363" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133517 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that the random variable $X$ has the probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x) = e^{-|2x|}, \\quad x \\in (-\\infty, \\infty),\n$$\n\nfind the cumulative distribution function $F(x).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133517 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "98dd834c-ac74-5cdd-88ee-060fd9ac689d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133517 #edb/ref 1074 "Since our random variable $X$ has a probability distribution function $f(x)$ that involves an absolute value, the cumulative distribution function takes the following form:\n\n![](images/40/40ebced9e322ef969e40733356aacdb3971e14276bc6ad54691aeea21b14c488.svg)\n\nLet's compute the integrals.\n\n- If $x \\leq 0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{x}{e}^{2t}\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\frac{{e}^{2t}}{2}|}_{-\\infty }^{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{2x}}{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}{e}^{2t}\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\frac{{e}^{2x}}{2}|}_{x=0} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- If $x > 0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{x}{e}^{-2t}\\,\\text{d}t & =-{\\frac{{e}^{-2t}}{2}|}_{0}^{x} \\\\  & =-\\frac{{e}^{-2x}}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}{e}^{2t}\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{0}^{x}{e}^{-2t}\\,\\text{d}t & =\\frac{1}{2}+(-\\frac{{e}^{-2x}}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =1-\\frac{{e}^{-2x}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the cumulative distribution function is given by\n\n![](images/92/9293ff0dab0e6c12fd143ec5f8a9d72edfce74fcddae2397f19d0e82a8b767cc.svg)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133518 #edb/ref 1069 "e-13364" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133518 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that the random variable $X$ has the probability density function\n![](images/9d/9d755ff833ca8a9134d8f2548b6e7e26221a4814cda8a28093a345a2d321920e.svg)\n\nfind the cumulative distribution function $F(x).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133518 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "4159a407-ea9f-5728-b219-bf1afab732a2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133518 #edb/ref 1074 "Since our random variable $X$ has a probability distribution function $f(x)$ that is strictly positive only for $0 \\leq x \\leq 12,$ the cumulative distribution function takes the following form:\n\n![](images/fd/fd6306e35f5c55b4de635fe7d8967e7783f11c96cee2ea8adfdd17348f97a095.svg)\n\nFor the given probability density function, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{x}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\int }_{0}^{x}\\frac{1}{72}t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\text{ } & ={\\frac{1}{144}{t}^{2}|}_{0}^{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{144}{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the cumulative distribution function is given by\n\n![](images/b1/b168cb9b6e4619e7b25cddea9ee671e665d057923b9562ff50d99ce874ea8b90.svg)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133519 #edb/ref 1069 "e-13365" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133519 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that the random variable $X$ has the cumulative distribution function\n![](images/97/9792a5033ccd5f6f8198e3465899c752ddd4ae0986b324c13575c1a92e1ff3c7.svg)\n\nfind $P(2\\leq X \\leq 6).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133519 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "707e3b49-9c13-50d8-8a7f-c4d5ec44e0ff" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133519 #edb/ref 1074 "Remember that for continuous random variables, it doesn't matter whether or not we include the endpoints of the interval. So we can perform this computation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(2\\le X\\le 6) & =P(2<X\\le 6) \\\\  & =P(X\\le 6)-P(X\\le 2) \\\\  & =F(6)-F(2) \\\\  & =\\frac{{6}^{2}-25}{75}-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{11}{75}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133726 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049638 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133726 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186134158 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133726 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186134538 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133726 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186419947 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133726 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186419952 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133726 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186419953 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133726 #edb/ref 1039 "Finding a CDF Given a PDF With a Lower Bound" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133726 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186133516 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133726 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "1d7509fa-a254-5b78-b307-3e0c4ef4d9ec" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133727 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186134159 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133727 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186134541 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133727 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186419950 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133727 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186419951 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133727 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186419956 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133727 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186419958 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133727 #edb/ref 1039 "Computing a CDF Given a PDF That’s Nonzero Over All Real Numbers" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133727 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186133517 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133727 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "7b0ce833-e045-5aff-94a4-f9ad56cdb904" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133728 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186134150 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133728 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186134160 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133728 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186419943 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133728 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186419949 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133728 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186419954 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133728 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186419955 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133728 #edb/ref 1039 "Finding a CDF Given a PDF With Lower and Upper Bounds" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133728 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186133518 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133728 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "eedcfa3d-b02b-5f96-99f4-cd0a452d272a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133729 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186134539 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133729 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186134540 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133729 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186419944 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133729 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186419945 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133729 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186419946 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133729 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186419948 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133729 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186419957 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133729 #edb/ref 1039 "Computing a Probability Over an Interval Using a CDF" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133729 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186133519 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133729 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "3cbc399b-90f3-5acd-ac70-7345a8436978" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134025 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186134578 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134025 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186134579 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134025 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186134580 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134025 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186134581 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134025 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186134582 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134025 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186134583 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134025 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186134584 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134025 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134025 #edb/ref 1045 "Cumulative Distribution Functions for Continuous Random Variables" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134025 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186134578 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134025 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186050750 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134025 #edb/ref 1050 1200.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134025 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "061434b6-5079-5b3b-b4da-2e73f02d7ea8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134159 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186131063 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134159 #edb/ref 1069 "q-210766" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134159 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134159 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that the random variable $X$ has the probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{{e}^{x}}{(1+{e}^{x})^{2}},\\,x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),\n$$\n\nfind the cumulative distribution function $F(x).$\n\n$F(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134159 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "1bbb59a5-e080-5eb3-8f00-497d7dc7343f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134159 #edb/ref 1074 "The cumulative distribution function of a continuous random variable $X$ is the function $F(x)$ defined by\n\n$$\nF(x)=P(X\\le x)={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{x}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nFor the given probability density function, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(x) & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{x}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{x}\\frac{{e}^{t}}{(1+{e}^{t})^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={-\\frac{1}{1+{e}^{t}}|}_{-\\infty }^{x} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{1+{e}^{x}}+1 \\\\  & =1-\\frac{1}{1+{e}^{x}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{x}}{1+{e}^{x}}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134578 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186134579 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134578 #edb/ref 1061 132590 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134578 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "3f773be7-dfa4-524f-aec7-8ade124c5cea" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134578 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186135186 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134579 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186134580 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134579 #edb/ref 1061 132591 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134579 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "2411aa47-0b30-5e9c-b541-4d44b5f7aeed" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134579 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186133726 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134580 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186134581 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134580 #edb/ref 1061 132594 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134580 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "f242a3a5-626f-54b6-b0cc-b8af4506a3f0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134580 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186133727 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134581 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186134582 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134581 #edb/ref 1061 132597 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134581 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "a74ce991-870f-5bfa-8a96-8bbed06ce5b6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134581 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186135187 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134582 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186134583 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134582 #edb/ref 1061 132598 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134582 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "47bc1334-f06b-5cc0-b191-427d1d862db4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134582 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186133728 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134583 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186134584 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134583 #edb/ref 1061 132601 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134583 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "55418ca8-74f9-5755-9f75-2d411c4a476e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134583 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186135188 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134584 #edb/ref 1061 132602 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134584 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "681dc276-df96-5b4d-b26c-338d9da62b88" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134584 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186133729 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135186 #edb/ref 1064 "The cumulative distribution function (or CDF) of a continuous random variable is defined the same way as for a discrete random variable. \n\nGiven a continuous random variable $X,$ the CDF is the function $F(x)$ such that\n\n$$\nF(x) = P(X \\leq x).\n$$\n\nIf our random variable has the probability density function (or PDF) $f(x),$ then we have\n\n$$\nF(x) = P(X \\leq x) = \\int_{-\\infty}^x f(t) \\, \\textrm dt.\n$$\n\nNote that we use $f(t)dt$ in the integrand because $x$ serves as the upper limit of integration. \n\nFor example, consider the random variable $X$ with the following PDF:\n\n![](images/e7/e79c230f2b548c9280425f555f6d99a8f997e989289a05fb5f4584b656c4dcc8.svg)\n\nSince $f(x)= 0$ for all $x < 0,$  we have\n\n$$\nF(x)  = \\int_{-\\infty}^x 0 \\, \\textrm dt = 0\n$$\n\nif $x<0.$\n\nTo compute the CDF for $x\\geq 0,$  we integrate $f(x)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(x) & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{x}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{0}^{x}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =0+{\\int }_{0}^{x}\\frac{2}{\\pi (1+{t}^{2})}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\frac{2}{\\pi }\\operatorname{arctan}⁡t|}_{0}^{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\pi }\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x-\\frac{2}{\\pi }(0) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\pi }\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpressing our CDF as a function defined over the entire real line, we get\n\n![](images/66/66b7bf54271075ae72fcf07fa4dc8573a94e97c2b2cc77680c0ec94b6053f360.svg)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135186 #edb/ref 1065 8364 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135186 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "de1bbdf6-687d-537a-a6fe-03d9fafbe841" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135186 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135187 #edb/ref 1064 "When a random variable $X$ has a probability distribution function $f(x)$ that is strictly positive on the interval $a \\leq x \\leq b$ only, we have\n\n- $P(X \\leq x) = 0$ for $x < a,$ and\n\n- $P(X \\leq x) = 1$ for $x > b.$\n\nIn these cases, the cumulative distribution function takes the following form:\n\n![](images/7a/7a64bac7c252a3f52fc92f6364edb5c66f31e47da28e78eeb0d069d894135700.svg)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135187 #edb/ref 1065 8365 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135187 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "0794419f-6b72-568b-a787-d71121c8bcd9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135187 #edb/ref 1067 "Finding a CDF Given a PDF With Lower and Upper Bounds" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135188 #edb/ref 1064 "As with discrete random variables, we can use the CDF of a continuous random variable to quickly compute a probability over an interval:\n\n$$\nP(a \\leq X \\leq b) = F(b) - F(a)\n$$\n\nFor example, suppose that a random variable $X$ has the following CDF:\n\n$$\nF(x) = \\dfrac{1}{1+e^{-x}}, \\qquad -\\infty < x < \\infty.\n$$\n\nWe can use the above CDF to quickly compute $P(0 \\leq x \\leq 1),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(0\\le x\\le 1) & =F(1)-F(0) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1+{e}^{-1}}-\\frac{1}{1+{e}^{0}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1+{e}^{-1}}-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & ≈0.231\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRemember that for continuous random variables, it doesn't matter whether or not we include the endpoints of the interval, so we have\n\n$$\nP(0 \\leq X \\leq 1) = P(0 < X \\leq 1) = P(0 \\leq X < 1) = P(0 < X < 1) = 0.231.\n$$\n\nIn general, for a continuous random variable $X,$ we have\n\n$$\nP(a \\leq X \\leq b) = P(a < X \\leq b) = P(a \\leq X < b) = P(a < X < b) = F(b) - F(a).\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135188 #edb/ref 1065 8366 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135188 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "e5203710-822b-55d4-9dfb-985bc4d41d6a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135188 #edb/ref 1067 "Using a CDF to Compute a Probability Over an Interval" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419875 #edb/ref 1080 "F(x)=\\frac{\\operatorname{arctan}⁡{e}^{x}-1}{\\pi }" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419875 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419875 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "4f0f787d-1687-5d7b-90fc-bcc440b2102f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419880 #edb/ref 1080 "F(x)=\\frac{2\\operatorname{arctan}⁡{e}^{x}}{\\pi }" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419880 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419880 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "6178be04-2216-5153-b323-5257d0136d60" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419896 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{{x}^{3}}{324}+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{108}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419896 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419896 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "9092706e-4579-52cd-b336-558d7e1b9f64" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419900 #edb/ref 1080 "F(x)=\\frac{2\\operatorname{arctan}⁡{e}^{x}}{\\pi }+\\frac{1}{2}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419900 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419900 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "9506108c-e704-538c-b725-308070b7259a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419908 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{e^{x}}{1+e^{x}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419908 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419908 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "b8dd2c37-ec9c-5602-ab5a-e63c24ab6b8b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419913 #edb/ref 1080 "F(x)=\\frac{2\\operatorname{arctan}⁡{e}^{x}}{\\pi }-\\frac{1}{2}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419913 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419913 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "c702e577-7142-5b26-9822-367c7f969cb4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419925 #edb/ref 1080 "F(x)=\\frac{\\operatorname{arctan}⁡{e}^{x}}{\\pi }" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419925 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419925 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "e9553f1f-e879-592c-9f68-96e639709428" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419933 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186419875 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419933 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186419880 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419933 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186419900 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419933 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186419913 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419933 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186419925 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419933 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419933 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419933 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186419880 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419933 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "7a631477-d8b0-5499-9e18-4bec118a663c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419941 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186419896 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419941 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419941 #edb/ref 1077 "field-1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419941 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186419896 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419941 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "fbd2268b-230b-5334-ac68-b3b22356482f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419950 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186419933 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419950 #edb/ref 1069 "q-89631" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419950 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419950 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that the random variable $X$ has the probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{2{e}^{x}}{\\pi (1+{e}^{2x})},\\,x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),\n$$\n\nfind the cumulative distribution function $F(x).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419950 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "aa0570cf-0a8d-5b19-8cfc-d1d338d11235" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419950 #edb/ref 1074 "The cumulative distribution function of a continuous random variable $X$ is the function $F(x)$ defined by\n\n$$\nF(x)=P(X\\le x)={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{x}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nFor the given probability density function, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(x) & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{x}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{x}\\frac{2{e}^{t}}{\\pi (1+{e}^{2t})}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\frac{2\\operatorname{arctan}⁡{e}^{t}}{\\pi }|}_{-\\infty }^{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\operatorname{arctan}⁡{e}^{x}}{\\pi }\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419955 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186419941 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419955 #edb/ref 1069 "q-210788" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419955 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419955 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that the random variable $X$ has the probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{108}x(x+2),\\, & 0\\le x\\le 6 \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nits cumulative distribution function is \n\n$$\nF(x)={\\begin{aligned}0,\\, & x<0 \\\\ h(x),\\, & 0\\le x\\le 6 \\\\ 1,\\, & x>6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $h(x)=${{field-1}}." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419955 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "dad3449e-8974-53d7-9ad6-1544954b39d1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419955 #edb/ref 1074 "Since our random variable $X$ has a probability distribution function $f(x)$ that is strictly positive only for $0\\le x\\le 6,$ the cumulative distribution function takes the following form:\n\n$$\nF(x)=P(X\\le x)={\\begin{aligned}0, & x<0 \\\\ {\\int }_{0}^{x}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t, & 0\\le x\\le 6 \\\\ 1, & x>6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the given probability density function, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{x}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\int }_{0}^{x}\\frac{1}{108}t(t+2)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\text{ } & ={\\frac{1}{324}{t}^{2}(t+3)|}_{0}^{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{3}}{324}+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{108}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the cumulative distribution function is given by\n\n$$\nF(x)={\\begin{aligned}0, & x<0 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{3}}{324}+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{108}, & 0\\le x\\le 6 \\\\ 1, & x>6.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
